   "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:75mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/pmt/pure-core-2-ch4/q02-fig-1.png\" alt=\"Figure 2: axes x and y with origin O; a curve that starts on the positive y-axis, dips slightly and then rises steeply; the region S under the curve between the y-axis and the vertical line at x = ln 4 is shaded; labelled &quot;Diagram not drawn to scale&quot;\"></figure><p>The finite region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span>, shown shaded in Figure 2, is bounded by the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span>-axis, the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span>-axis, the line with equation <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x=\\ln4\"></span> and the curve with equation</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=\\mathrm e^x+2\\mathrm e^{-x},\\qquad x\\geqslant0\" data-display=\"true\"></div><p>The region <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S\"></span> is rotated through <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"2\\pi\"></span> radians about the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"x\"></span>-axis.</p><p>Use integration to find the exact value of the volume of the solid generated.<br>Give your answer in its simplest form.</p><p>[<em>Solutions based entirely on graphical or numerical methods are not acceptable.</em>] <span class=\"marks\">(7)</span></p>",
